## Phase 2 — Semantic Expansion
**Goal:** Build the missing content layer. Add pages the guide requires. Establish internal linking.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> Do not start Phase 2 until Phase 1 is complete and verified. Phase 2 pages depend on correct internal link targets from Phase 1 pages.

### 2.1 Platform Pages (Feature Pages)

The guide calls for `/platform/` pages. These explain the operational system components — not marketing copy. Think: accountability systems, visibility tools, consistency mechanisms.

**Pages to create:**

| Route | Primary topic | Connects to |
|---|---|---|
| `/platform/proof-based-audits` | What proof-based audits are, why they matter, how they work | All solutions pages |
| `/platform/issue-tracking` | How issue tracking works end-to-end | Solutions + corrective actions use-case |
| `/platform/corrective-actions` | Corrective action workflows, SLA system | Solutions pages |
| `/platform/operational-dashboards` | What visibility looks like from each role | Solutions pages |
| `/platform/geo-verified-audits` | Geo-verification, why it matters | Solutions pages |

**Page structure per the guide (Commercial SEO Page layers):**
1. Discovery match — what this feature is
2. Operational pain — why it matters at scale
3. Hidden failure — what happens without it
4. Operational system — how Audiment solves it
5. Operational outcome — what you can do now

**Tone:** Operationally mature. Not a feature spec sheet. Not startup copy. These pages should feel like product documentation written by someone who understands operations.

**AEO requirement on every platform page:**
- Minimum one question-based section heading (e.g. "What is a proof-based audit?")
- A concise direct answer block (2–4 sentences, no jargon)
- FAQ schema block (3–5 questions)

---

### 2.2 Operational Problem Pages

The guide explicitly requires these. They are not feature pages — they describe operational realities that operators recognize.

**Pages to create:**

| Route | What it covers |
|---|---|
| `/operational-drift` | What operational drift is, how it happens silently, how to detect and stop it |
| `/hidden-operational-issues` | Why problems stay hidden until escalation, what enables that, what prevents it |
| `/maintaining-standards-across-locations` | The core challenge of multi-location consistency |
| `/staying-ahead-of-compliance` | Proactive vs. reactive compliance, FSSAI context |
| `/improving-accountability` | Accountability systems, what breaks down without them |

**These pages serve two purposes:**
1. AEO: AI systems retrieve these when users ask "how do multi-location businesses maintain consistency" type questions.
2. Internal link targets: Every commercial solutions page should link to the relevant operational problem page.

**Page structure:**
- H1: The operational problem, stated plainly (e.g. "Operational Drift in Multi-Location Businesses")
- What it is (concise definition)
- How it happens (operational reality, not theory)
- What it costs (business impact, no hype)
- How audit systems address it
- Internal link to relevant platform page + relevant solutions page
- FAQ block

**Tone check:** These pages should feel like something an operations director would recognize immediately. No startup voice. No "game-changing solutions."

